{
  "schema": "mnrh-corridor-definitions/1",
  "comment": "offset_mm and textbook_direction are in the image's RAS axes, not the APP frame: x = patient right(+)/left(-), y = anterior(+)/posterior(-), z = cephalad(+)/caudal(-). An offset is added to the named landmark's position; entry/exit regions are a sphere (landmark + offset_mm, radius_mm) intersected with the surface of restrict_to_label. side: 'per_side' values are given for the RIGHT side, where +x is lateral; the LEFT instance mirrors x -> -x. A 'reference_side' of 'opposite' takes the landmark and the bone of the other side, which is how a transiliac-transsacral screw started on one side reaches the other. crosses_si_joint marks the corridors that cross the sacroiliac joint; how much of that joint counts as bone is measured per patient (DECISIONS.md section 2), not set here. Anchors are initial estimates, not yet reviewed against real anatomy. tip: 'inside' keeps the whole screw tip in bone with its full margin (targets are sampled in bone interior, lengths round down); 'through' lets the tip pass the far cortex near the target by up to one 5 mm catalogue step (targets on the far surface, lengths round up), DECISIONS.md 1.5-1.6. clear_of_fracture_mm keeps that end of the screw at least that far on the midline side of a marked fracture, so the screw crosses the fracture rather than starting past it (the surgeon marks the site; with nothing marked the rule does not apply). The posterior_column_triangle view is the single view of Sikarinkul et al. (Bangkok Med J 2025;21(2):116-122): obturator oblique 10 degrees with inlet 25 degrees, which shows the corridor bounded by the quadrilateral surface medially, the retro-acetabular surface laterally and the supero-posterior acetabular surface below.",
